======================================================================
APP/DATA - SAMPLE DATASETS
======================================================================
Small test extracts for the card estate.  Two kinds of file:

  *.ebc  *.vb   mainframe files, EBCDIC cp037, binary.  Do not open these
                in a text editor and do not let git or a file transfer
                translate them.
  *.csv         ASCII companions for the DB2 seed tables.

Everything here is synthetic.  Card numbers are built on the standard test
BINs (400000, 411111, 510510, 555555, 601100) and carry valid mod-10 check
digits so they exercise the validation, but they are not issuable and no
real cardholder, merchant or sanctioned entity appears anywhere in these
files.

Regenerate the lot with:

    cd app/data
    python3 mkdata.py

The generator holds the data as literal tables and writes the same bytes
every time, so a regenerated file is byte for byte identical to the one in
the repository.  Nothing here is random and nothing here is derived from
production.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
1.  WHY THE BINARY FILES ARE BINARY
----------------------------------------------------------------------
The .ebc and .vb files are the point of this directory.  They carry the
three things that a naive conversion destroys:

  cp037 encoding    Every character byte is EBCDIC.  'A' is X'C1', a blank
                    is X'40', the digit 0 is X'F0'.

  packed decimal    COMP-3 fields hold two digits per byte with the sign in
                    the low nibble of the last byte.  84.19 in a
                    S9(11)V99 COMP-3 field is X'00000000841 9C'.  Those
                    bytes are not characters in any code page and a
                    translating transfer corrupts them silently.

  zoned overpunch   Signed DISPLAY fields carry the sign as the zone of the
                    last byte.  +841.9 ends X'C9', which translates to the
                    letter I; -841.9 ends X'D9', which is R.  A file that
                    has been through an ASCII conversion looks almost
                    right, which is worse than looking wrong.

Transfer them BINARY.  If you are moving them to z/OS:

    quote site rdw            (for the .vb files only)
    binary
    put txnpost.vb 'CARD.TEST.TXNPOST'

.gitattributes in the repository root should keep git from touching them.
If a diff on one of these ever shows text, something has translated it.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
2.  FILE INVENTORY
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  file            recs   RECFM  LRECL      layout        records
  --------------  -----  -----  ---------  ------------  ---------------
  cardmast.ebc      22   FB       179      CVCARD01Y     card master
  acctmast.ebc      18   FB       169      CVACCT01Y     account master
  authextr.ebc      20   FB       179      CVAUTH01Y     auth extract
  glpost.ebc        24   FB       182      GL interface  GL postings
  txnpost.vb        31   VB    233-497     CVTRAN01Y     posted txns
  stmtarch.vb       15   VB   284-24204    CVSTMT01Y     statement archive
  --------------  -----  -----  ---------  ------------  ---------------
  accounts.csv      18                                   CARDSVC.ACCOUNT
  cards.csv         22                                   CARDSVC.CARD
  limits.csv        20                                   CARDSVC.CARD_LIMIT
  merchants.csv     20                                   CARDSVC.MERCHANT
  fee_schedule.csv  20                                   CARDSVC.FEE_SCHEDULE
  fraud_rules.csv   12                                   CARDSVC.FRAUD_RULE
  customers.csv     19                                   PARTYRSK.CUSTOMER
  parties.csv       12                                   PARTYRSK party view
  kyc.csv           14                                   PARTYRSK.PARTY_KYC
  sanctions.csv     18                                   PARTYRSK.PARTY_SANCTION

The VB files carry a four byte RDW on every record - two bytes of length
including the RDW itself, then two bytes of zero.  There is no BDW; the
records are unblocked as they sit here.  The LRECL column above is the
record length without the RDW, which is what the copybook describes.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
3.  THE BINARY FILES IN DETAIL
----------------------------------------------------------------------

3.1  cardmast.ebc - CVCARD01Y, FB 179
-------------------------------------
One record per card, ascending on CARD-NUM as the file is written.

CARD-ACTIVATION-DT is the six digit legacy date.  It has to be windowed
against WS-CENTURY-PIVOT in CVCONSTY: a YY of 50 or more is 19YY, below 50
is 20YY.  Card 4000000000002081 carries 981105, which windows to 1998 -
read it as 2098 and the card is not yet issued.  Every other activation
date in the file is in the 2000s, so a program that gets the windowing
wrong looks correct on twenty one of the twenty two records.

3.2  acctmast.ebc - CVACCT01Y, FB 169
-------------------------------------
One record per account.  Six COMP-3 balance fields in ACCT-BALANCES and a
seventh in ACCT-DELQ-AMT.  Account 40000000118 is written off with a
delinquency bucket of 6 and its whole balance in ACCT-DELQ-AMT; account
40000000113 is suspended at bucket 4.  The rest are current.

3.3  authextr.ebc - CVAUTH01Y, FB 179
-------------------------------------
Twenty authorizations, all on 2024-07-12.  This is the file to test the
variant handling against.

The 60 byte AUTH-DETAIL area is redefined three ways and the three variants
are different lengths:

    AUTH-TYPE 'P'  AUTH-PURCHASE   60 bytes, fills the area
    AUTH-TYPE 'C'  AUTH-CASH-ADV   36 bytes, 24 bytes of tail left over
    AUTH-TYPE 'R'  AUTH-REFUND     39 bytes, 21 bytes of tail left over

The generator writes the variants into a single 60 byte buffer and never
clears it between records, which is what the program that produced the real
extract does - the area is WORKING-STORAGE and only the variant fields are
moved into it.  So the tail of every short record holds the tail of
whatever record was written before it.  Record 3 is a cash advance and its
last 24 bytes are the end of the purchase in record 2.

Those bytes are not padding and they are not blanks.  Any code that reads
past the end of the variant it is handling reads the previous record.  The
file is ordered so that all three variants follow each of the others at
least once.

Sequence numbers 1 to 20 in AUTH-SEQ-NUM, in the order the records appear.
Fourteen approvals, three declines, two referrals and one reversal.

3.4  glpost.ebc - FB 182
------------------------
The interface file to the general ledger, laid out to match
CARDSVC.GL_POSTING.  There is no copybook for this one in app/cpy - the GL
owns the layout and it has not changed since the eighties:

    offset  len  field
         1   12  GL batch id
        13    9  GL sequence number, zoned unsigned
        22    8  posting date CCYYMMDD
        30    8  value date CCYYMMDD
        38   10  GL account
        48    6  cost centre
        54    1  debit/credit indicator
        55   15  amount, SIGNED ZONED, two implied decimals
        70    3  currency
        73   16  source transaction id
        89   11  source account id
       100   40  narrative
       140    8  cycle id
       148    1  reversal flag
       149    8  posted by
       157   26  posting timestamp

The amount at offset 55 is the signed zoned field.  Credits are negative
and their last byte carries a D zone.  Debits and credits in this extract
do not balance to zero on their own - the contra entries are on the
settlement side and are not part of this file.

3.5  txnpost.vb - CVTRAN01Y, VB 233-497
---------------------------------------
Thirty one posted transactions.  TXN-LEG is an OCCURS 1 TO 12 DEPENDING ON
TXN-LEG-CNT, and TXN-TRAILER follows the table, so the trailer is at a
different offset in every record with a different leg count.  A program
that addresses the trailer at a fixed offset reads leg data instead and
does not abend.

Leg counts present: 1 (eleven records), 2 (nine), 3 (five), 4 (two), and one
record each at 5, 6, 8 and 12.  TXN0000000000031 is the twelve leg record -
the maximum the ODO allows, 497 bytes, and the one to size a buffer against.

Every record is 209 bytes plus 24 bytes per leg.

3.6  stmtarch.vb - CVSTMT01Y, VB 284-24204
------------------------------------------
Fifteen statements for the July 2024 cycles.  STMT-LINE is an OCCURS 1 TO
300 DEPENDING ON STMT-LINE-CNT with the trailer after it, same shape of
problem as the transaction file but three orders of magnitude wider: the
shortest record is 284 bytes and the longest, the 300 line statement for
account 40000000117, is 24204.

Line counts run 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 14, 17, 22, 26, 31, 47, 118 and 300.
Anything that reads this file needs a 24K record area; a 4K one handles
fourteen of the fifteen records.

Every record is 204 bytes plus 80 bytes per statement line.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
4.  THE CSV COMPANIONS
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Plain ASCII, comma separated, one header row, LF line endings, no quoting
beyond what the values force.  Dates are ISO CCYY-MM-DD and empty where the
column is null; the one exception is CARDS.ACTIVATION_YYMMDD, which is the
six digit legacy value as it is actually stored.

They are the same data as the binary files, so a row in accounts.csv
reconciles against the matching record in acctmast.ebc, and every card in
cards.csv appears in cardmast.ebc.  Referential integrity holds across the
set: every account points at a real customer and party, every card at a
real account, every KYC row at a real party.

For a DB2 LOAD you will want DELIMITED format with CHARDEL '"' and COLDEL
',', and the CCSID conversion doing ASCII to EBCDIC on the way in.  The
column order in each file matches the column order in db2/ddl.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
5.  THE AWKWARD CASES
----------------------------------------------------------------------
These are in the data on purpose.  They are the cases the estate really
carries, and anything that reads this data has to survive them.

  a party holding several customer numbers
      PTY00000004 has four rows in customers.csv - 400000104, 400000105,
      400000106 and 400000119 - across three product segments, one of them
      dormant.  Party is the identity; the customer number is not.  Code
      that assumes one customer per party silently processes a quarter of
      this party's relationships.

  a name that disagrees between the card and the customer
      Card 4111110000001078 is embossed ROBERT D MCALLISTER.  Its customer
      record, 400000107, has the surname as MACALLISTER, and so does the
      party record.  Both spellings are in use; neither is a data error
      anyone is going to fix.  A join on name finds nothing.

  a merchant with no route entry
      MRC000000000019 PIONEER SQUARE POPUP MARKET has SETTLE_ROUTE_CD
      blank.  Authorization 17 and transaction TXN0000000000017 are against
      it, so the settlement lookup for that transaction finds nothing.

  a six digit date that windows to the previous century
      Card 4000000000002081, activation 981105.  See 3.1.

  the maximum leg count
      TXN0000000000031, twelve legs.  See 3.5.

  a sanctions near miss
      Customer 400000104 is MERRIWEATHER, JONATHAN ROSS, date of birth
      1957-02-11.  OFAC entry SDN0000000000412 is MERIWEATHER, JONATHON,
      same date of birth.  One R, one vowel, and the dates of birth agree,
      which is what makes it a referral rather than a discard.  Its party
      PTY00000004 correspondingly has an open event driven KYC review at
      sequence 2 with status PN.

      There are three other deliberate near misses of decreasing quality:
      SZABO, ILDIKO against customer SZABO, ILONA KATALIN (same date of
      birth, different given name); VANDERBEEK, PETER J against
      VANDERBECK, PIETER JAN; and DELACROIX, SIMON against
      DELACROIX-BENNETT, SIMONE.  PETROVA, YELENA on the EU list is an
      exact name and date of birth match against customer 400000111, and
      NAKAMURA, HIROSHI is on the internal list, matching the customer
      whose card is blocked and whose account is suspended.

Other things worth knowing about the set: card 5105100000001058 is blocked
for fraud with two failed PIN tries, card 5105100000001165 is reported lost
with three, account 40000000106 is closed with a card that expired in 2000,
and card 4000000000001190 has never been activated so its activation and
last used dates are zero rather than blank.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
6.  THE GENERATOR
----------------------------------------------------------------------
mkdata.py, Python 3, standard library only.  It writes the files listed in
section 2 into its own directory and overwrites what is there.

The record layouts in it were transcribed by hand from the copybooks named
in section 3.  It does not read the COBOL source, so a copybook change does
not reach it: if a layout moves, the transcription in the generator has to
be changed to match, and there is nothing that will warn you.  The field
encoders (alnum, zoned, zoned_signed, comp3) are at the top of the file and
are the only place the encoding rules live.
======================================================================
